  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected\nFriday, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the mid-Atlantic region results in dry \nconditions today. Low pressure to the northwest in Canada \ntracks eastward, bringing a cold front over the state Friday \ninto Saturday. Latest trend in guidance has PWATs roughly \n1.25-1.75 inches, with higher values concentrated around the \nCentral Highlands and Downeast regions. Models continue to hint \nat precipitation generally moving in early Friday from the \nnorthwest, and moving to the southeast by Friday night. There is\nstill some model discrepancies in the end timing, however \nimproving agreement with the latest 12z operational runs. QPF is\nnot that impressive with this system with most locations only\nseeing up to a quarter of an inch. Therefore, little if any\nimpact on the ongoing D1-D3 drought conditions. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain is possible Monday \nfollowed by gusty westerly winds on Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSunday night a cold surface high pressure that will have \nbrought widespread frost Sunday AM shifts to the east over the\nMaritimes. A potent vertically stacked low pressure system\ntracks into north-central Quebec with a trailing potent cold\nfront. That front will swing across the CWA on Monday at the \nsame time as a low pressure exits off the Mid-Atlantic coast. \nThe interaction between the high pressure and the two lows \nprovides significant uncertainty about rainfall. A few scenarios\ncamps currently playing out in the ensemble models. 1. The high\npressure could dominate forcing the lows to stay to the north \nand south, limiting precip. 2. The Canadian low could enhance \nthe coastal low as it rides the coast north, bringing more \nsubstantial precip to coastal areas. 3. The Canadian low could \ndominate, bringing enhanced precipitation along its cold front \nfrom the moisture over the southeast low. At this time, \nuncertainty from the models is high, so forecast confidence is \nlow, with a wide range of precipitation values. \n\nIncreasing confidence that Tuesday as the low pressure system\ndeepens in the Northern Maritimes we can expect strong surface\nhigh to dive over the eastern Great Lakes. This will lead to a\nstrong pressure gradient between the low and high resulting in\ngusty W-WNW winds developing in the morning and peaking midday.\nProbabilities for 35+ mph wind gusts have increased to 60-80%\nwith 40+ mph probabilities now 35-55% which have come up\nsignificantly in the last 24 hours modeling. There is ensemble\nguidance suggesting a potential of wind headlines but still\nuncertainty exists on exacted expected wind speeds/gusts.\nHowever, it is likely that significant leaf loss may be possible\nin areas of Northern Maine where peak foliage has been reached.\nAdditional leaf loss in the Central Highlands to Downeast Maine\nwhere the foliage is moderate to high. Continue to monitor the\nforecast for updates, as models begin to get a better handle on\nthe atmospheric set up.",
